-- title:
--   Unique place at the j=0 node with prescribed crossing value
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $q \ge 5$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$ whose zero set is exactly the maximal ideal of $A$, and assume $0 \in$ `ssJSet q k`, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $k$ with $j$-invariant $0$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$. Let $K \subset \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be a finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, put $O = A \cap K$ (`coeffSubring A K`) with the induced reduction map, and let $\varpi \in O$ generate the kernel of that reduction, with $q = \varpi^{e_K}\varepsilon$ for a unit $\varepsilon$ and $e_K \ge 1$. Work in the ring $R$ of Laurent series $f$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ admitting $f\cdot\mathrm{ev}(s) = \mathrm{ev}(r)$ with $r,s \in O[X_0,X_1]$ and $s$ not vanishing at the point $(0,0^q)$, where $\mathrm{ev}$ sends $X_0, X_1$ to the two $j$-series of level $1\cdot q$ (`modularLocalizedAtPoint`). Let $G',H',w \in R$ with $w$ a unit, $G'H' = \varpi^{\,\mathrm{jWidth}(0)\cdot e_K}\,w$ (so the exponent is $3e_K$), the maximal ideal of $R$ equal to $(\varpi,G',H')$, the ideals $(\varpi,G')$ and $(\varpi,H')$ prime with $H' \notin (\varpi,G')$ and $G' \notin (\varpi,H')$, and $(\varpi,G') = (\varpi, \mathrm{ev}(X_1-X_0^q))$, $(\varpi,H') = (\varpi,\mathrm{ev}(X_0-X_1^q))$. Then for every element $G_f$ of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $1\cdot q$ whose underlying Laurent series is $G'$, and every $c$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ admitting $m$ in that maximal ideal with $cm = q^3$, there is exactly one place $W$ of that function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (a valuation subring containing the base field, proper, with principal ideals) which is centred at the node, in the sense that $\mathrm{ord}_W(j_q - x) > 0$ for some $x \in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\,x = 0$ and $\mathrm{ord}_W(\mathrm{qExpand}(j_q) - y) > 0$ for some $y \in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\,y = 0^q$, and at which $G_f$ has value $c$, i.e. $G_f$ lies in the valuation subring of $W$ and reduces to the image of $c$ in its residue field.
--
--   This is the statement that the crossing parameter $G'$ is injective, with prescribed image, on the tube of width $\mathrm{jWidth}(0) = 3$ around the supersingular node $j = 0$ of $X_0(q)$: each admissible value $c$, namely each $c$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ whose complementary factor in $q^3$ also lies there, is attained at exactly one place centred at the node. It is used to compute the order of vanishing at such places and to produce the supersingular annulus centred at $j = 0$ used in the level-lowering argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_NodeLocalized_existsUnique_place_centred_zero_hasValue_of_crossingPresentation.lean

